PATCH 486: FUN_0000_a17e's point-struct parameters were 16-bit (every caller's HOST pointer truncated),
and its third caller FUN_1000_6507 -- the nearest-OBJECTIVE scan -- was fully base-lost.

a17e is the `call 0x8e8; lret` forwarder of the 32-bit distance helper; SI/DI are the two point structs
08e8 dereferences.  Ghidra typed them `undefined2`, so the host pointers 9efc (target acquisition, patch
483) and 9d49 (patch 473) pass were cut to their low word and 08e8 dereferenced a 16-bit stub address:
INDIA1's self-play SEGV'd on its first target candidate with `FUN_0000_08e8(0x62a0,0x44fa)` (AZER1 hid it
-- its units rarely pass 9efc's earlier gates, only one held a target lock).  Widened to `int`, as patch
367 already did for the sibling forwarder a19a.

6507 (asm 0x16507-0x1658a) ranks the mission OBJECTIVES by distance: the goal-group table word[DGROUP:
0x7d2a+g] for g=0,2,4,6 (plus 8..0xe when byte[0x6dab]&2), each entry a near-offset list whose first byte
is the count and whose 8-byte entries start at +0xc, measured against the reference point DGROUP:0x7bf0;
the winner lands in word[0x7bf8]/[0x7bfc]/[0x7bfe].  Ghidra host-deref'd the list, used the uninitialised
`unaff_CS` as the entry index and took the distance from `extraout_AH`, so the AI's objective ranking was
garbage -- and differed between -O0 and -O2.
